The current position has no immediate winning move for \(x\) (Player 1) and no immediate threat that must be blocked for \(o\) (Player 2). Consequently the optimal strategy is to claim the most valuable remaining square that simultaneously prepares multiple forced-win patterns while denying \(o\) the same resource.

Column 4 is that square. Dropping in column 4 places an \(x\) at cell \((3,4)\). This single stone:

* creates a vertical pair with the already-present \(x\) at \((4,4)\), so that a later stone at \((2,4)\) produces an immediate vertical threat;
* completes the base of the diagonal window \((5,3)\)-\((4,4)\)-\((3,5)\)-\((2,6)\);
* finishes the horizontal window \((3,3)\)-\((3,4)\)-\((3,5)\)-\((3,6)\).

Because of gravity the two windows share the same follow-up column (column 6). Consequently any line that obtains the stone at \((3,5)\) while column 6 contains exactly two stones produces a forced win:

* the horizontal three threatens \((3,6)\);
* \(o\)’s obligatory block at \((3,6)\) opens \((2,6)\), which completes the diagonal four on the next move.

All of \(o\)’s plausible replies after the move to column 4 can be answered by the same family of races (contesting columns 5 and 6 while retaining a latent forcing move on the first row). In every variation \(x\) can arrange the necessary parity on those two columns and therefore finish with one of the four-in-a-rows above. No other legal column creates a comparable double-threat infrastructure, so column 4 is the unique optimal choice.

Final Answer: 4

